.Dd Jul 14, 2026
.Dt XTC_CSKIP 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm xtc_cskip_create ,
.Nm xtc_cskip_destroy ,
.Nm xtc_cskip_get ,
.Nm xtc_cskip_insert ,
.Nm xtc_cskip_remove ,
.Nm xtc_cskip_min ,
.Nm xtc_cskip_floor ,
.Nm xtc_cskip_size
.Nd RCU-protected concurrent ordered map (skiplist)
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In xtc.h
.In xtc_cskip.h
.In xtc_rcu.h
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_cskip_create "xtc_cskip_cmp_fn cmp" "xtc_cskip_t **out"
.Ft void
.Fn xtc_cskip_destroy "xtc_cskip_t *s"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_cskip_get "xtc_cskip_t *s" "const void *key" "void **out_value"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_cskip_insert "xtc_cskip_t *s" "void *key" "void *value" "void **out_old_value"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_cskip_remove "xtc_cskip_t *s" "const void *key" "void **out_value"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_cskip_min "xtc_cskip_t *s" "void **out_key" "void **out_value"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_cskip_floor "xtc_cskip_t *s" "const void *key" "void **out_key" "void **out_value"
.Ft size_t
.Fn xtc_cskip_size "const xtc_cskip_t *s"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm xtc_cskip
is a concurrent ordered map -- a skiplist
.Pq Pugh 1990
built on
.Xr xtc_rcu 3
so that lookups are lock-free, walking the tower with acquire-ordered
loads and no lock at all, while writers still support concurrent
readers.  It is the
.Em ordered
sibling of
.Xr xtc_chash 3 :
where the hash table answers only
.Dq is this exact key present ,
.Nm xtc_cskip
additionally keeps keys in sorted order, so it answers
.Dq what is the smallest key
.Pq Fn xtc_cskip_min
and
.Dq what is the largest key <= K
.Pq Fn xtc_cskip_floor ,
the predecessor / range queries an unordered hash table cannot serve.
.Pp
Keys and values are caller-owned
.Vt void *
pointers stored verbatim, the same convention as
.Xr xtc_chash 3 ,
.Xr xtc_chan 3 ,
and
.Xr xtc_pdict 3 :
the map never copies or frees a key or value.  A caller-supplied
comparator
.Fa cmp
imposes the ordering; unlike
.Xr xtc_chash 3 ,
which only tests equality,
.Nm xtc_cskip
uses the FULL
.Xr qsort 3
ordering
.Pq <0 , 0 , >0 ,
so
.Fa cmp
MUST be a total order over the key space.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_cskip_create
allocates an empty map.
.Fn xtc_cskip_destroy
frees the map's own node bookkeeping; it does not free caller-owned
keys/values and is not safe to call concurrently with any other call
on the same map -- quiesce first, exactly as
.Xr xtc_rcu 3 Ns 's
.Fn xtc_rcu_fini
requires.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_cskip_get ,
.Fn xtc_cskip_min ,
and
.Fn xtc_cskip_floor
must be called inside the caller's own
.Fn xtc_rcu_read_lock Ns / Ns Fn xtc_rcu_read_unlock
bracket
.Pq nesting is fine .
Each writes its result pointer(s) on a hit and returns
.Dv XTC_OK ,
or returns
.Dv XTC_E_NOTFOUND
on a miss / empty map; the returned pointers are valid only until the
caller's read-side ends.
.Fn xtc_cskip_min
reports the entry with the smallest key;
.Fn xtc_cskip_floor
reports the entry with the largest key
.Fa <= key
.Pq and Dv XTC_E_NOTFOUND if no key is <= Fa key .
Any of the
.Fa out_*
arguments may be NULL if the caller does not want that half.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_cskip_insert
and
.Fn xtc_cskip_remove
take the read-side internally; the caller does not need an outer
read-side.
.Fn xtc_cskip_insert
replaces any existing entry for an equal key, writing the old value
pointer to
.Fa *out_old_value
.Pq NULL for a fresh insert; the argument itself may be NULL .
.Fn xtc_cskip_remove
writes the removed value pointer to
.Fa *out_value
.Pq may be NULL
and returns
.Dv XTC_E_NOTFOUND
on a miss.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_cskip_size
returns the approximate live entry count -- exact with no concurrent
writers.
.Sh CONCURRENCY AND RECLAMATION
Readers are lock-free.  Writers
.Pq Fn xtc_cskip_insert , Fn xtc_cskip_remove
serialize on a single per-map mutex in this version -- the reader path
is the one that must scale, and it takes no lock.  A finer-grained
per-node writer lock is a deliberate deferred optimization
.Pq recorded in the source ;
it is not needed until a workload shows writer contention.
.Pp
An insert links its new node bottom-up, level 0 first: the base level
is the source of truth for membership, so the node is present the
instant its level-0 link is published
.Pq a single release store ,
and the upper levels are only a search accelerator a reader tolerates
seeing half-built.  A remove unlinks top-down by overwriting each
predecessor's forward pointer with a release store of the removed
node's own
.Pq unchanged
successor, then hands the node to
.Fn xtc_rcu_retire
-- never freed synchronously.  A reader already parked on the node
keeps walking its untouched forward pointers until it leaves its
read-side; RCU reclaims the node only after every such reader has
drained.
.Pp
This module never calls
.Fn xtc_rcu_synchronize
itself; as with every other
.Xr xtc_rcu 3
consumer, reclamation of retired nodes happens on the caller's, a
test's, or an application-installed reaper's schedule.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Dv XTC_OK
on success;
.Dv XTC_E_INVAL
for a NULL map/key or a missing comparator at create;
.Dv XTC_E_NOTFOUND
on a get/remove/min/floor miss;
.Dv XTC_E_NOMEM
on allocation failure.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
static int
i64_cmp(const void *a, const void *b)
{
	int64_t x = *(const int64_t *)a, y = *(const int64_t *)b;
	return x < y ? -1 : (x > y ? 1 : 0);
}

int64_t k1 = 10, k2 = 20, k3 = 30;
xtc_cskip_t *s;
xtc_cskip_create(i64_cmp, &s);
xtc_cskip_insert(s, &k3, "thirty", NULL);
xtc_cskip_insert(s, &k1, "ten", NULL);
xtc_cskip_insert(s, &k2, "twenty", NULL);

xtc_rcu_read_lock();
void *mk, *mv;
if (xtc_cskip_min(s, &mk, &mv) == XTC_OK)
	printf("min key %ld -> %s\en", (long)*(int64_t *)mk, (char *)mv);
int64_t q = 25;
void *fk, *fv;
if (xtc_cskip_floor(s, &q, &fk, &fv) == XTC_OK)
	printf("floor(25) -> %ld=%s\en", (long)*(int64_t *)fk, (char *)fv);
xtc_rcu_read_unlock();

xtc_cskip_destroy(s);
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr xtc_rcu 3 ,
.Xr xtc_chash 3 ,
.Xr xtc_chan 3 ,
.Xr xtc_pdict 3 ,
.Xr xtc 7
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Nm xtc_cskip
ordered map first appeared in libxtc 1.21.
